WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.590
<v Instructor>So we have seen a lot of different components</v>

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.460
so far and now it's time to just take a step back and

3
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.710
compare all the kind of options we have

4
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.710
when we do solution architecture,

5
00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.230
just with the web layer and the application layer.

6
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.760
So we are going to compare EC2 with its own

7
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.263
Elastic IP, EC2 with Route53,

8
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:23.173
ALB + ASG, ALB + ECS on EC2, ALB + ECS on Fargate,

9
00:00:25.253 --> 00:00:28.510
ALB + Lambda, API Gateway + Lambda,

10
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.490
API Gateway with AWS Service and then finally

11
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.840
API Gateway with an HTTP backend, for example an ALB.

12
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.090
So let's get started.

13
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.380
First EC2 with Elastic IP.

14
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.710
So we have our user talking to our public instance

15
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.360
and has a public IP and an elastic IP attached to it

16
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.080
and with an elastic IP address.

17
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.820
And what we want to do is have some kind of failover.

18
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.870
So we're going to have a standby instance

19
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.560
and this standby instance is going to be there.

20
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.740
We want to failover.

21
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.100
So in case we want to failover we're going to have

22
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.448
the elastic IP address move all the way to the second

23
00:01:00.448 --> 00:01:04.280
EC2 instance in case of the elastic recovery.

24
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.490
Then our user will be redirected automatically

25
00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.260
because they've been using the elastic IP address

26
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.290
to the standby instance.

27
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.180
That is very classic.

28
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.130
It provides you quick failover.

29
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.210
The client should not see the change happen

30
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.880
because it can make with the same elastic IP address.

31
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.190
It's very helpful if the client needs to resolve only

32
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.960
by static public IP address.

33
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.182
But it does not scale because you can only have one

34
00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:28.970
EC2 instance at any time serving traffic.

35
00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.330
And on top of it, it's cheap.

36
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.800
So that's one solution architecture and it's not too bad.

37
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.930
Okay so next we have stateless web app.

38
00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.540
We want to scale them horizontally.

39
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.630
So let's say we have three EC2 instances.

40
00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.808
They're public, but there's no elastic IP this time

41
00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:45.650
and we have users.

42
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.750
What the users will do is that they will perform

43
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:51.450
a DNS query on Route53 and they will get back

44
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.680
an A record and thanks to this A record,

45
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.290
they're going to be able to talk to our EC2 instances.

46
00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.060
But what happens if one of these EC2 instances

47
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.320
is being terminated?

48
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.764
For example in which case,

49
00:02:02.764 --> 00:02:05.610
some of our users will not be able to reach

50
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.350
our application and that can be a problem.

51
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.340
So in this case, we are to download something called

52
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.730
DNS-based load balancing.

53
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.856
Because based on the DNS results, we are getting

54
00:02:14.856 --> 00:02:18.660
different answers and this is how load balancing works.

55
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.620
So here we are able to use multiple instances.

56
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.587
And Route53 TTL implies that some clients

57
00:02:24.587 --> 00:02:26.850
may get outdated information.

58
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.510
So even though we're able to have a health check

59
00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.310
for example, it will take some time,

60
00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.550
maybe one hour in this case

61
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.770
because that's where the TTL is for our users to be

62
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.490
redirected to the correct instances.

63
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.960
Finally the clients may have some logic to deal

64
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.630
with hostname resolution failures,

65
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.280
for example if somehow one host name does not work,

66
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.657
it may be interesting for us to do something,

67
00:02:46.657 --> 00:02:50.940
which is to redo a DNS query, force it, and resolve

68
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.850
the new DNS results to try another URL.

69
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.350
Finally I think an instance if you do that

70
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.990
with its own same scheme they may not receive full traffic

71
00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:01.490
right away due to the DNS TTL.

72
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.970
Again, you need to wait for the new clients to re-resolve

73
00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.490
the DNS for a new instance to receive some traffic.

74
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.460
So this works fine.

75
00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.230
This is architecture that the both often choose,

76
00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.040
but it's not perfect.

77
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.750
Okay, then we have ALB + ASG.

78
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.840
So here's something that we've seen a thousand times

79
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.500
where we have our Route53 DNS query pointing as an

80
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.620
Alias Record to an ALB.

81
00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.920
The ALB will have health checks, multi AZ enabled,

82
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.310
and then will be routing to three availability zones,

83
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.620
five instances that are living in an auto scaling group.

84
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.800
And so we've seen this as a very classic architecture

85
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.980
on AWS that scales very well.

86
00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.900
The new instances that are added to our auto scaling group

87
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.760
will be in service right away because the load balances

88
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.570
will be routing traffic directly to them

89
00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.190
and the users will never be sent to instances

90
00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.160
that are out-of-service.

91
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.730
Thanks to the health checks, this is very beneficial.

92
00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:57.260
The time to scale though is pretty quite slow because

93
00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.867
in case you add EC2 instances you need to wait for the

94
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:03.720
EC2 instance to start up, then there is the EC2 user data

95
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.920
bootstrap script and somehow if you use an AMI

96
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.280
that has all these things clear already,

97
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.380
it can help speed up the time to scale.

98
00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.160
But it's quite slow I would say,

99
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.394
we're talking about a few minutes,

100
00:04:14.394 --> 00:04:15.280
but that's okay,

101
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.130
that's very proper for many types of architecture.

102
00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.470
The ALB is elastic, but it cannot handle sudden

103
00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.260
huge peak of demands, okay, so you need to pre-warm it

104
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.700
if you except a massive load happening very, very soon.

105
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.550
And in case some instances are overloaded,

106
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.100
it's possible that you're losing a request,

107
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.161
meaning that your clients have to retry some requests

108
00:04:35.161 --> 00:04:38.110
over time in case they don't through.

109
00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.530
CloudWatch can be used for scaling the auto scaling group.

110
00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.927
And we've seen that there is Cross-Zone load balancing,

111
00:04:43.927 --> 00:04:47.429
if you want to evenly distribute across all AZ

112
00:04:47.429 --> 00:04:50.010
from the load balancer.

113
00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.330
Okay and so just a real world tip,

114
00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.140
which is that if you're trying to scale

115
00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.140
your auto-scaling group

116
00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.910
a good target utilization rise off CBU,

117
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.630
a good number would be within 40% to 70% depending

118
00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.800
on how you feel about your application.

119
00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.770
Okay, next we have ALB + ECS on EC2

120
00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.970
that is backed by an auto scaling group.

121
00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.450
So this is extremely similar to what we had before.

122
00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.000
It has the exact same properties as the discussion

123
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.500
we just had before when we just had an ALB and an ASG,

124
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.120
but now instead of having the task of instance run

125
00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.010
the application itself, we have ECS tasks on the instances.

126
00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.500
So the application has to be run on Docker

127
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.276
and because we're using ECS it is possible

128
00:05:31.276 --> 00:05:34.240
to use a dynamic port mapping feature

129
00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.370
to run multiple tasks on the same application

130
00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.820
on EC2 instance,

131
00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.137
potentially helping us maximize the utilization

132
00:05:40.137 --> 00:05:42.300
of an EC2 instance.

133
00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.330
The thing is though with this architecture as we discussed

134
00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.836
already is that it's really really tough to orchestrate

135
00:05:46.836 --> 00:05:51.310
ECS service auto-scaling alongside ASG auto-scaling.

136
00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.190
So it is very possible that you will need to have two sets

137
00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:57.020
of auto-scaling rules in place to scale both your

138
00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.820
EC2 instances that will run your tasks and your service

139
00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.540
itself that will run the tasks as well.

140
00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.737
So the answer to this is to use ECS on Fargate.

141
00:06:05.737 --> 00:06:08.360
In this case we don't have any auto-scaling group.

142
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.480
We don't have any EC2 instances to manage,

143
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.280
we have Fargate that has service auto-scaling.

144
00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.320
And the tasks will be launched on the AWS network

145
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:17.760
automatically for you,

146
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.700
and we don't need to manage that at all.

147
00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.110
So the application again is run on Docker.

148
00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.640
Service auto-scaling is going to be very easy.

149
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.495
The time to be in service is going to be very very

150
00:06:26.495 --> 00:06:28.840
quick, so we don't need to launch an EC2 instance

151
00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:29.673
in advance.

152
00:06:29.673 --> 00:06:32.350
The only thing we need to launch is a Docker container.

153
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.210
So it could be really really quick to launch

154
00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:35.320
a Docker container.

155
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.040
YOu're still limited by the ALB in case of sudden peaks

156
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.060
of request.

157
00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.868
And the things we've made serverless in this instance

158
00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.640
is the application tier.

159
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.410
I don't like to call ALB serverless,

160
00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.810
I like to call it managed, because it doesn't scale

161
00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.020
as nicely, but still it's a very nice architecture,

162
00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.073
something very common that scales really well

163
00:06:54.073 --> 00:06:56.720
that is no management or that requires no management

164
00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:58.880
on your side and is going to be very safe and

165
00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.390
very scalable.

166
00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.300
Next we're going to the serverless world.

167
00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.820
So we have Lambda.

168
00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:05.247
So we can use the application of ECS

169
00:07:05.247 --> 00:07:08.792
alongside Lambda so we see that target groups of an ALB

170
00:07:08.792 --> 00:07:10.450
could be Lambda.

171
00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.500
In which case, we're going to be limited

172
00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.560
by Lambda's runtime.

173
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.410
Remember we have specific client runtimes

174
00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:17.300
and we can also have customer runtimes.

175
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.930
But in case we won't have Docker

176
00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.590
it's more and more access

177
00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.411
recommended to use ECS instead,

178
00:07:22.411 --> 00:07:24.484
but we're going to have seamless scaling

179
00:07:24.484 --> 00:07:26.420
thanks to the Lambda runtime.

180
00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.810
It's going to be very helpful.

181
00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.050
Remember when we have 1000 Lambda functions

182
00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.010
that be convertly executed,

183
00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.940
it's going to be a very very simple way

184
00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.220
to expose your Lambda function as HTTP or HTTP/S

185
00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.730
without using all the features from API Gateway.

186
00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.201
And as you can expect you get a lot of cost saving

187
00:07:42.201 --> 00:07:46.700
factors like 20X or 10X by using the ALB + Lambda,

188
00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.560
but you don't have the features of the API Gateway.

189
00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.252
The cool thing is that you can combine this

190
00:07:51.252 --> 00:07:53.230
with the Web Application Firewall

191
00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.310
for security on your ALB,

192
00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:58.050
and it's good for hybrid microservices.

193
00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.639
As an example, you can use ECS for some requests

194
00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:02.720
and use Lambda for other requests,

195
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:03.990
depending on the type of workload,

196
00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.330
your Docker container, or if it's a very simple function.

197
00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.900
This is a very fine solution architecture.

198
00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.750
One other thing that is lesser known on AWS,

199
00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.580
but extremely helpful.

200
00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.250
Otherwise, the one that is more known

201
00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:20.090
is going to be when you have API Gateway and Lambda.

202
00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.800
In which case, you eventually pay for requests,

203
00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.060
seamless scaling, fully serverless.

204
00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.710
You need to be aware of soft limits, 10,000 requests

205
00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.300
per second on your API Gateway,

206
00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.030
and 1000 concurrent in execution but both these things

207
00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.520
are sublimits and you can increase that.

208
00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.015
Because you have an API Gateway we get more features

209
00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:40.890
such as authentication, rate limiting, caching,

210
00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:44.270
API keys and all the things we've seen from before.

211
00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.910
Lambda Cold Start time may increase some latency

212
00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:48.590
for some requests especially if you start

213
00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.410
as well chaining up a bunch of these architectures together.

214
00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.230
We've seen this, but the good thing is that it's

215
00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.210
fully integrated with X-Ray, so we can get

216
00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.250
full tracing of all the requests

217
00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:59.170
made within your infrastructure.

218
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.862
Finally we see the API Gateway can be used

219
00:09:01.862 --> 00:09:04.700
with AWS Service as a proxy.

220
00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.422
An okay architecture would be to do this.

221
00:09:07.422 --> 00:09:10.051
It's not the best, where you have the clients,

222
00:09:10.051 --> 00:09:12.800
API Gateway and then Lambda, and Lambda will be

223
00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.482
for example putting a message into an SQS queue.

224
00:09:15.482 --> 00:09:18.750
The better architecture would be to have the client

225
00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.930
talk to the API Gateway and the API Gateway

226
00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.080
be integrated with SQS directly.

227
00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.940
So we definitely would do this, number one,

228
00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.360
because we get lower latency and it's cheaper.

229
00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.750
We avoid invoking Lambda and it makes more sense.

230
00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.440
We're also not using Lambda concurrent capacity.

231
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.380
There's no custom code to maintain

232
00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.550
and we are exposing AWS API securely

233
00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.050
for the API Gateway which is what it was

234
00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.830
intended to be in the first place.

235
00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.550
So this could not be joined only SQS, it could be SNS,

236
00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.100
Step Functions and so on.

237
00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:47.844
One thing to remember though is an API Gateway

238
00:09:47.844 --> 00:09:51.110
has a payload limit of 10 MB and so it can be

239
00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.277
a problem if you're trying to have an API Gateway

240
00:09:53.277 --> 00:09:55.780
as a proxy for S3.

241
00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.997
Okay, lastly, API Gateway with HTTP backend

242
00:09:58.997 --> 00:10:00.760
and for example an ALB.

243
00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.626
So we have this architecture and we would use this

244
00:10:03.626 --> 00:10:06.190
for getting the API Gateway features on top of a

245
00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:07.450
custom HTTP backend.

246
00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.030
So we would want the authentication, the rate control,

247
00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:12.272
API keys, caching, et cetera, et cetera.

248
00:10:12.272 --> 00:10:14.217
It can connect to on-premise servers,

249
00:10:14.217 --> 00:10:15.730
an application load balancer,

250
00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.570
or even a third party HTTP service.

251
00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:20.830
Okay, so that's it, we've visited all the ways

252
00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:22.612
to make a web layer and a compute layer so far.

253
00:10:22.612 --> 00:10:24.530
I hope everything makes sense.

254
00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:26.670
I hope these discussions of solution architecture

255
00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.213
are bringing your thinking forward and I will see you

256
00:10:29.213 --> 00:10:30.433
in the next lecture.

